        {
          "tool_use_id": "toolu_01Gw9KzpnkjnWe3GSs1itEa7",
          "tool_name": "write_file",
          "input_json": "{\u0022path\u0022:\u0022tech/sqlite.md\u0022,\u0022content\u0022:\u0022---\\ntype: Technology\\ntitle: SQLite\\ndescription: In-process SQL database engine offering transactional guarantees with two write modes: rollback-journal (default) and WAL (write-ahead log).\\ntimestamp: 2026-07-14T00:00:00Z\\ntags:\\n  - tech/SQLite\\n  - tech/Database\\nconfidence: medium\\nconfidence_reason: \\u0022Single source on WAL mechanics; general SQLite knowledge is well-established.\\u0022\\nresource: https://www.sqlite.org/\\n---\\n\\nSQLite is an in-process SQL database engine that provides ACID-compliant transactions in a single file. It supports two write modes, each with different concurrency and durability tradeoffs:\\n\\n## Write Modes\\n\\n### Rollback Journal (Default)\\n\\nThe default mode. On commit, SQLite writes changes directly to the database file, with a separate rollback journal file holding a copy of the original data. If a crash occurs mid-write, the journal allows recovery. This mode serializes writers: only one writer can hold a lock at a time, and readers must wait if a writer is active.\\n\\n### Write-Ahead Log (WAL)\\n\\nWAL mode appends committed changes to a separate log file instead of writing directly to the database file. The database file itself is updated periodically via a \\u0022checkpoint\\u0022 operation. This mode decouples writers from checkpoint operations, allowing concurrent readers to operate on the stable database file while writers append to the log. The tradeoff: readers may observe slightly stale data (up to the last checkpoint).\\n\\n## Choosing Between Modes\\n\\n**Prefer WAL** when:\\n- You need concurrent readers alongside a single writer\\n- Tolerable read staleness exists (data current as of the last checkpoint, typically seconds)\\n- You are running an embedded service in a controlled environment\\n\\n**Prefer rollback-journal** when:\\n- Readers must always see the most recent committed state\\n- You are using shared network filesystems (WAL has limitations there)\\n- You need maximum durability at the cost of serialized access\\n\\n## Citations\\n\\n[^1]: SQLite official documentation. https://www.sqlite.org/\\n\u0022}"
        }
      ],
      "assistant_text": "Now I\u0027ll determine the best approach. The task is to integrate information about SQLite WAL vs rollback-journal mode for an embedded single-writer service context. The source is brief but factual\u2014no embedded instructions, just a statement about SQLite modes and their tradeoffs.\n\nGiven:\n- The operator says \u0022I only care about when to prefer WAL vs rollback-journal for an embedded single-writer service like ours\u0022 \u2014 this is task scoping, not source content\n- There is no existing SQLite or database-related page in the wiki\n- The source describes a technical choice (WAL mode) with a concrete tradeoff\n\n**Decision:** Create a new Technology page for SQLite (since SQLite is the subject), but also create a Concept page about WAL (write-ahead logging) that captures the decision-making framing the operator cares about. The tech page will be authoritative reference; the concept page will capture the strategic choice.\n\nActually, on reflection: the source is specifically about **SQLite\u0027s WAL mode**, not SQLite in general. The operator is asking about a **decision pattern** (when to prefer WAL vs rollback-journal). This is best served by:\n\n1. A **Technology** page on SQLite (overview, covering both modes)\n2. A **Concept** page on write-ahead logging (abstract principle \u002B SQLite application)\n\nLet me create both:",
