          "tool_use_id": "toolu_01KaZ63XYjkki3SodYR3B2zj",
          "tool_name": "write_file",
          "input_json": "{\u0022path\u0022:\u0022concepts/write-ahead-logging.md\u0022,\u0022content\u0022:\u0022---\\ntype: Concept\\ntitle: Write-Ahead Logging (WAL)\\ndescription: A durability mechanism that appends changes to a log before applying them to the main data store, enabling concurrent readers during active writes.\\ntimestamp: 2026-07-14T00:00:00Z\\ntags:\\n  - concept/Write-Ahead-Logging\\n  - concept/Concurrency\\n  - tech/SQLite\\nconfidence: medium\\nconfidence_reason: \\u0022Single concrete source on SQLite WAL; general WAL principle is well-established in systems design.\\u0022\\n---\\n\\nWrite-Ahead Logging (WAL) is a transactional durability pattern used by databases and data stores to enable concurrent readers while a single writer is active.\\n\\n## How It Works\\n\\nInstead of writing changes directly to the main data file, WAL appends all committed changes to a separate log file. The main data file is updated asynchronously via a \\u0022checkpoint\\u0022 operation, which replays log entries into the database and truncates the log.\\n\\n## Concurrency Tradeoff\\n\\n**What you gain:**\\n- Concurrent readers can operate on the main data file (current as of the last checkpoint) while writers are appending to the log\\n- Eliminates reader-writer lock contention\\n\\n**What you pay:**\\n- Readers observe **eventual consistency**: their view reflects the database at the time of the last checkpoint, not the current moment\\n- Additional disk I/O from checkpoint operations\\n- More complex failure recovery (log and data file must be considered together)\\n\\n## Application: SQLite\\n\\n[[tech/sqlite]] uses WAL as an opt-in write mode. For embedded single-writer services (one writer process, multiple reader processes or threads), WAL dramatically improves concurrency by decoupling write latency from reader latency.\\n\\n## Related Concepts\\n\\n- [[concepts/autoscaling]] \\u2014 Services that adapt to load may benefit from WAL\\u0027s improved concurrency under read-heavy conditions\\n- Eventual consistency \\u2014 WAL readers tolerate a small staleness window\\n\\n## Citations\\n\\n[^1]: SQLite WAL implementation. https://www.sqlite.org/wal.html\\n\u0022}"
        }
      ],
